<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jean Le Rond d'Alembert</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1717">1717</a>–<a href="/years/1783">1783</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a> · Also known as d'Alembert</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jean Le Rond d'Alembert was a French mathematician, physicist, philosopher and music theorist who was co-editor of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/encyclopedie">Encyclopédie</a> with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denis-diderot">Denis Diderot</a> until 1759. Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> as the natural son of the writer Claudine Guérin de Tencin, he was left by his mother on the steps of the church of Saint-Jean-le-Rond, after which he was named, and raised by the wife of a glazier. He was elected to the Académie des Sciences in 1741 and in 1743 published his Traité de dynamique, in which he set out his own laws of motion. He wrote over a thousand articles for the Encyclopédie, including its Preliminary Discourse, and is known for the wave equation and the formula that solves it.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jean-le-rond-dalembert.jpg" alt="Jean Le Rond d'Alembert" width="330" height="408" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: After Maurice Quentin de La Tour · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jean_Le_Rond_d'Alembert,_by_French_school.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
